From: cap-bot <cap-bot@discord.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 22:50:01 +0000
Subject: fix: use sRGB gamma and contrast boost for Linux text

Chromium builds Skia on Linux with SK_GAMMA_EXPONENT=1.2 and
SK_GAMMA_CONTRAST=0.2, and without SK_GAMMA_APPLY_TO_A8. Skia's FreeType
backend skips gamma and contrast entirely for grayscale (A8) glyphs unless
SK_GAMMA_APPLY_TO_A8 is defined, so grayscale text on Linux is blended with
no correction at all. Light text on dark backgrounds looks thin and washed
out on low-DPI displays as a result.

Match the Windows tuning (sRGB gamma, contrast 1.0, which Chromium has
shipped as the Windows default since M132) and apply it to grayscale glyphs
as well. ChromeOS keeps the upstream values.

diff --git a/skia/BUILD.gn b/skia/BUILD.gn
index a60e48a67d9ebebf3d189ac645bc90c87b2a3703..49f4dc116313ca5d0c23d9eeabfcc776bd9e668f 100644
--- a/skia/BUILD.gn
+++ b/skia/BUILD.gn
@@ -110,7 +110,15 @@ config("skia_config") {
 
   # `SK_GAMMA_EXPONENT` and `SK_GAMMA_CONTRAST` are settings for text
   # blitting, chosen to approximate the system browser.
-  if (is_linux || is_chromeos) {
+  if (is_linux) {
+    # Match the Windows tuning (sRGB gamma, contrast 1.0) and apply it to
+    # grayscale glyphs too, which FreeType otherwise leaves uncorrected.
+    defines += [
+      "SK_GAMMA_APPLY_TO_A8",
+      "SK_GAMMA_SRGB",
+      "SK_GAMMA_CONTRAST=1.0",
+    ]
+  } else if (is_chromeos) {
     defines += [
       "SK_GAMMA_EXPONENT=1.2",
       "SK_GAMMA_CONTRAST=0.2",
